--- machine\generic\authn\policy
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy

Machine policy for the privileges, integrity, owner, and default access rules
of issued tokens.

Each subkey names a principal. Token grants combine the applicable user and
group records; one record alone does not describe all of a principal's
access. Resource descriptors and existing tokens also affect access.

The key belongs under `Generic` because another authentication authority
can implement the same policy. Source registration belongs separately under
`Machine\Software\Authd\Sources`.

If this key exists, its records are the complete source of privilege grants.
The compiled privilege floor is not merged into them. If the key is absent,
the floor grants `SeChangeNotifyPrivilege` to `Everyone` and nothing else.
That fallback alone does not guarantee successful sign-in or remove access
granted by group membership and resource descriptors.

An unreadable key grants no fallback privileges. Treating it as absent would
restore a grant that machine policy might have removed. authd logs the
failure. authd reads at most 256 principal records and warns about extras.

Without an explicit descriptor, the key inherits from its parent. The
shipped Machine hive policy grants SYSTEM and Administrators full access,
and Authenticated Users read access. Check the effective descriptor before
relying on these defaults.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy deniedprivileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy DeniedPrivileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (none)
valid: privilege names; an unknown name is dropped with a warning
applies: live (at the next logon)

Privileges excluded from the grants computed for a token.

The authority combines privileges from applicable principal records, then
removes this set. This lets an administrator exclude a privilege without
editing every record that grants it. The value does not remove privileges
from tokens already issued.

The value belongs on the parent policy key, separate from principal records.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy logonsocketdescriptor
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy LogonSocketDescriptor
type: REG_SZ
default: O:SYG:SYD:P(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)(A;;0x100082;;;AU)
valid: SDDL; a malformed value falls back to the built-in
applies: restart

Security descriptor controlling connections to `/run/logon.sock`.

The value uses security descriptor definition language (SDDL). The default
grants SYSTEM and Administrators full access and Authenticated Users connect
access. That permits signed-in accounts to request self-service credential
changes and session ending, subject to each operation's authorization.

Connecting does not grant logon origination. For a non-SYSTEM originator,
`Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> LogonTypes` must also permit the
requested type for the peer token's user security identifier (SID). If the
socket already admits that peer, only the originator grant needs changing.
SYSTEM can originate every supported logon type without a policy record.

A replacement descriptor that omits an account can prevent its self-service
operations. authd reads this value at startup and applies it when binding
the socket; editing the registry does not restamp a running socket.

This value does not configure `/run/psi.sock` or `/run/ident.sock`. Their
descriptors are fixed by authd. All three sockets use protected descriptors
to prevent inherited access from changing the intended grants.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy sessionendsecurity
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy SessionEndSecurity
type: REG_SZ
default: O:SYG:SYD:(A;;0x1;;;SY)(A;;0x1;;;BA)
valid: SDDL; a malformed value lets nobody end another's session
applies: live

Security descriptor authorizing requests to end another account's session.

The value uses security descriptor definition language (SDDL). authd checks
the caller's token for access right `0x1`; every generic right maps to it.
The default grants SYSTEM and Administrators that access.

Through `/run/logon.sock`, an authorized client asks authd to end a session.
authd sends `SIGTERM` to processes whose primary tokens belong to it, then
`SIGKILL` after a grace period. Scans are bounded, and processes can remain.
The kernel destroys the session when its last token reference is released.
See PGSS Logon §2.22 and the event `authd.session.ended`.

An issued-domain account can end its own eligible personal session without
this access check. No caller can use this operation to end SYSTEM's session
(999), Anonymous's session (998), or a service session. Stop services through
the service manager.

A present value that is unreadable, empty, incorrectly typed, or invalid
SDDL denies requests to end another account's session. authd logs the
problem instead of restoring the default grant. The value is read for each
check, so an edit affects the next applicable request.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal>
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal>

Policy grants associated with one principal's security identifier (SID).

The key name is a bare well-known name or literal SID. Well-known names
include `Everyone`, `Administrators`, `Users`, `SYSTEM`, `Local Service`,
`Network Service`, `Authenticated Users`, `Guests`, `Anonymous`, `Local`,
`Domain Admins`, and the logon-type groups. Matching is case-insensitive.
`BUILTIN\Administrators` cannot be one subkey name because the backslash
separates registry keys. Use `Administrators` or its literal SID.

Local account and group names are not resolved here. Use their literal
SIDs. authd warns and ignores a key name that identifies no known principal.

Token policy considers the user and applicable group SIDs, including groups
derived from the logon type. Values combine independently:

- `Privileges` combines the grants from applicable records.
- `Integrity` uses the user's value, or the highest stated group value.
- `Owner` and `DefaultDacl` use the user's value, or an unambiguous group
  value. Conflicting group choices fall back to their defaults.
- `LogonTypes` authorizes origination using only the requesting peer's user
  SID. It does not combine group grants.

An absent value leaves that part of the decision to the other applicable
records and documented defaults. A service-specific SID can receive token
policy grants. For origination, that SID must be the peer's user SID; merely
carrying it as a group does not match `LogonTypes`.

Avoid duplicate records for one SID. Privilege grants can combine, but
single-value decisions can depend on record enumeration order. authd warns
about duplicates; combine them into one record.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> privileges
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Privileges
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent — this record grants no privileges)
valid: Se...Privilege names; unknown names dropped with a warning
applies: live (at the next logon)

Privileges granted and turned on in a token matching this principal.

The authority combines grants from applicable records, then subtracts
`Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy DeniedPrivileges`. An empty list grants
nothing; an absent value contributes no privilege grant from this record.

Use full, case-sensitive privilege names such as `SeBackupPrivilege`.
An unknown name is dropped with a warning without rejecting other names.
Check diagnostics after editing the value to detect misspelled grants.

`SeCreateTokenPrivilege`, `SeTcbPrivilege`, and
`SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege` belong to the trusted computing base (TCB).
Do not grant them to ordinary accounts. They authorize operations that can
bypass ordinary authentication and process-identity boundaries.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> integrity
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Integrity
type: REG_SZ or REG_DWORD
default: Medium (compiled in unconditionally)
valid: Untrusted, Low, Medium, High, System — or any DWORD
applies: live (at the next logon)

Integrity level selected for a token matching this principal.

Use a named level or a numeric `REG_DWORD`. Names match case-insensitively;
the kernel compares levels numerically. An unrecognized name is ignored
with a warning, leaving other applicable records or the default to apply.

The default is Medium, including when the whole policy key exists. The
user's own value takes precedence over group values. Otherwise, the highest
stated group level applies. A group therefore cannot impose a minimum on a
user whose own record selects a lower level.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> owner
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> Owner
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — the principal owns what they create)
valid: a well-known name or a SID; unresolvable warns and is ignored
applies: live (at the next logon)

Default owner for objects created with a token matching this principal.

The value names a principal rather than an index into a token. The selected
SID must be carried by the token; otherwise authd warns and uses the token's
user as owner. The value does not transfer ownership of existing objects.

The user's record takes precedence. Otherwise, a group record can select
an owner. If applicable groups select different owners, authd warns and
falls back to the user. Multiple groups selecting the same owner agree.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> defaultdacl
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> DefaultDacl
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — this record supplies no default DACL)
valid: SDDL; a malformed value warns and is ignored
applies: live (at the next logon)

Default discretionary access control list (DACL) for newly created objects.

This list supplies access rules when an object has no parent inheritance or
other explicit descriptor supplying them. It uses security descriptor
definition language (SDDL), which lets administrators inspect the grants.

The kernel copies the token's default DACL without substituting
`CREATOR OWNER`. Use `OWNER RIGHTS` (`S-1-3-4`) to grant the actual owner.
The shipped policy places `D:(A;;GA;;;S-1-3-4)(A;;GA;;;SY)(A;;GA;;;BA)` on
`Everyone`.

An absent or malformed value contributes no default DACL from this record.
If no applicable record supplies one, the token has no default DACL. An
object created without another descriptor or parent inheritance can then
receive a null DACL, which grants unrestricted access. Check diagnostics
after editing this value.

The user's value takes precedence. Otherwise, groups can supply an agreed
value; conflicting group choices fall back to no default DACL.

--- machine\generic\authn\policy\<principal> logontypes
canonical: Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy\<principal> LogonTypes
type: REG_MULTI_SZ
default: (absent — this principal cannot originate logons)
valid: seven supported logon-type names; unknown names dropped with a warning
applies: live (at the next logon)

Logon types the peer can request on another account's behalf.

The supported names are `Interactive`, `Network`, `Batch`, `Service`,
`NetworkCleartext`, `NewCredentials`, and `RemoteInteractive`. Names are
case-sensitive. Unknown names are dropped with a warning.

This value controls the requesting process, not the account being signed
in. The source separately states which logon types the target account
permits. Both restrictions apply.

The record matches the socket peer token's user security identifier (SID)
only. Group memberships do not contribute. A service SID works here when
it is the user SID of a virtual service account, not merely a service group
on another account's token.

SYSTEM can originate every supported logon type without a record. For
other callers, a missing record, absent value, or empty list grants no
origination. Connection permission is separately controlled by
`Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy LogonSocketDescriptor`.

--- machine\software\authd
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd

Configuration specific to the authd authentication authority.

The `Sources` subkey controls principal-source registration. General token
and originator policy belongs under `Machine\Generic\Authn\Policy`.

--- machine\software\authd\sources
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources

Principal sources permitted to register with authd.

Each subkey names a service. authd derives its service security identifier
(SID) and checks that the connecting peer token carries it. A process's
claimed source name does not establish its identity.

An empty or absent key permits no source registration. authd considers at
most 64 entries and warns about extras. A missing registration prevents the
source from answering identity and authentication requests.

The allowlist is read at startup and during source registration. Accepted
connections retain their configuration. Editing or deleting a key does not
immediately revoke an existing connection; plan a source reconnection when
changing its registration policy.

Without an explicit descriptor, this key inherits from its parent. The
shipped Machine hive policy grants SYSTEM and Administrators full access,
and Authenticated Users read access. Write access controls which services
can assert identity to authd.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source>
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source>

Registration policy for the service named by this subkey.

The subkey permits registration. Its values configure the accepted domain,
numeric range, search order, and foreign-membership grant. These are not
all restrictions: a numeric range and foreign memberships grant authority.

authd derives the service security identifier (SID) from the key name and
checks the connecting token. An unusable name is ignored with a warning.
Values are optional; their individual records describe absence and invalid
values. The shipped `lpsd` seed sets `MayAssertForeignMemberships`,
`UnixIDBase`, and `UnixIDCount`.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> searchorder
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> SearchOrder
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1000
valid: any; lower is consulted first
applies: live (at the next source registration)

Source priority when resolving an unqualified name.

Lower values are consulted first. Equal values are ordered by source name,
so registration timing does not choose which principal a bare name means.
An earlier unavailable source prevents falling through to a different
principal in a later source.

A non-`REG_DWORD` value produces a warning and uses 1000. The accepted
priority belongs to the registered connection; reconnect the source to
change it.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> domain
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> Domain
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — unpinned, which is not unrestricted)
valid: a SID; malformed means this source cannot register
applies: live (at the next source registration)

Expected domain security identifier (SID) for source registration.

A source declares its domain during registration. authd confines its
identity assertions to that domain. This value compares the declaration
with an administrator's expected SID; the declaration alone is not proof
that the source owns a domain.

Without a pin, authd still requires the `S-1-5-21-A-B-C` domain shape and
rejects a domain held by another registered source. It also remembers each
source's first declared domain for the lifetime of the authd process.
Reconnecting cannot change that remembered domain. The in-memory check does
not survive an authd restart; a registry pin persists.

A malformed pin prevents registration instead of being treated as absent.
authd never writes this value. A connected source keeps the configuration
accepted at registration; an edit does not revoke its connection.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> unixidbase
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> UnixIDBase
type: REG_DWORD
default: (absent — no range, so principals project to nobody)
valid: 1000000 or above; below that is refused
applies: live (at the next source registration)

Base added to source-relative Unix identifiers.

For example, a base of 1,000,000 plus relative identifier 1000 produces
Unix identifier 1,001,000. lpsd starts account and group allocation at 1000.
It stores relative identifiers, so changing the base does not rewrite those
records. The registration acknowledgment tells the source its accepted
base and count; authd performs the projection for tokens and identity
results.

Values below 1,000,000 are unusable because that band is reserved for the
authority's well-known, service, and confinement identities. Configure
disjoint ranges for different sources. Bounds prevent an asserted relative
identifier from escaping its configured range; they do not correct an
administrator's overlapping range assignments.

An absent, malformed, or unusable range gives source principals the fallback
number 65,534 (`nobody`). It does not assign a guessed range. Inspect source
diagnostics and the effective identifiers after changing registration.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> unixidcount
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> UnixIDCount
type: REG_DWORD
default: 1000000
valid: 1 or more; base + count must not overflow
applies: live (at the next source registration)

Size of the numeric range assigned to a principal source.

A relative identifier must be greater than zero and less than this count.
The authority adds `UnixIDBase` only after validating those bounds. A count
of 1 therefore contains no usable relative principal identifier.

A non-`REG_DWORD` count produces a warning and uses 1,000,000. A zero count
or overflowing base-plus-count makes the whole range unusable, giving
principals the fallback number 65,534. Configure disjoint ranges for
separate sources; the bounds do not detect overlapping assignments.

--- machine\software\authd\sources\<source> mayassertforeignmemberships
canonical: Machine\Software\Authd\Sources\<source> MayAssertForeignMemberships
type: REG_DWORD
default: 0
valid: non-zero to set; absent or 0 is off
applies: live (at the next source registration)

Permission to assert group memberships outside the authenticated domain.

Without this grant, a source can assert memberships only in the same domain
as the principal it authenticated. The local source uses the grant to
assert local policy memberships, including built-in groups.

This permission does not allow authentication of an identity outside the
source's own domain. Grant it only to a source trusted to assert those
memberships. Otherwise a source could place its users into administrative
groups on this machine.

An absent value, zero, or a non-`REG_DWORD` leaves the permission off. A
registered source retains its accepted grant until its connection ends.

--- machine\system\locale
canonical: Machine\System\Locale

Machine-wide language and formatting preferences for personal sessions.

A locale selects language and conventions for dates, numbers, currency,
and sorting. Language packs supply installed locales; `C.UTF-8` is the
fallback. Session clients read this key when constructing `LANG` and
category-specific `LC_*` environment values.

Values under `Users\<SID>\Locale` override matching machine values. An
invalid or unavailable user value leaves the valid machine value in place.
Changing `LANG` does not clear separately stated category values. Existing
process environments do not change when the registry is edited.

The resolver reads only `LANG` and the documented categories. It ignores
`LC_ALL`, `LANGUAGE`, and `LOCPATH` values in this key. Services do not
receive these preferences automatically.

Without an explicit descriptor, the key inherits from its parent. The
shipped Machine hive policy grants SYSTEM and Administrators full access,
and Authenticated Users read access.

--- machine\system\locale lang
canonical: Machine\System\Locale LANG
type: REG_SZ
default: C.UTF-8
valid: an installed locale name, such as en_GB.UTF-8
applies: live (when a session starts)

Default locale for the machine's personal sessions.

Valid category-specific values under this key override `LANG` for their
categories. A valid user `LANG` replaces the machine `LANG`, but does not
clear machine category overrides. Invalid or unavailable values leave the
resolver's `C.UTF-8` fallback in place.

--- machine\system\locale lc_<category>
canonical: Machine\System\Locale LC_<category>
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — LANG applies)
valid: an installed locale name; the categories are LC_CTYPE, LC_NUMERIC, LC_TIME, LC_COLLATE, LC_MONETARY, LC_MESSAGES, LC_PAPER, LC_NAME, LC_ADDRESS, LC_TELEPHONE, LC_MEASUREMENT and LC_IDENTIFICATION
applies: live (when a session starts)

Machine locale override for one formatting or language category.

For example, `LC_TIME` set to `en_GB.UTF-8` selects British date formatting
while `LANG` set to `en_US.UTF-8` selects the general language. A valid user
value for the same category replaces it. A user `LANG` alone does not.

The documented categories exclude `LC_ALL`, `LANGUAGE`, and `LOCPATH`.
Those values are not read from registry locale preferences.

--- users\<sid>\locale
canonical: Users\<SID>\Locale

Language and formatting preferences for one account's personal sessions.

The key uses the same values as `Machine\System\Locale`. A valid installed
locale replaces the matching machine value. An absent or unusable category
value leaves the machine category in force, if one exists; otherwise the
selected `LANG` applies.

authd prepares `Users\<SID>` for the account at sign-in. The intended user
registry descriptor permits the account and administrators to change these
preferences. A change affects later session environments, not processes
already running.

--- users\<sid>\locale lang
canonical: Users\<SID>\Locale LANG
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — the machine's applies)
valid: an installed locale name, such as en_GB.UTF-8
applies: live (when a session starts)

General locale selected by this account for its personal sessions.

A valid installed locale overrides the machine `LANG`. An absent or
unusable value leaves the machine's general locale, or `C.UTF-8` if none is
usable. Separately stated machine or user category values still apply.

--- users\<sid>\locale lc_<category>
canonical: Users\<SID>\Locale LC_<category>
type: REG_SZ
default: (absent — machine category, or selected LANG, applies)
valid: an installed locale name; the categories are those of Machine\System\Locale
applies: live (when a session starts)

Account-specific locale for one formatting or language category.

A valid installed locale overrides the same machine category. An absent or
unusable value leaves that machine category in place. If neither layer has
a usable category value, the selected user or machine `LANG` applies.
